# Digest 12: pattern 5, Am I harming the child's development by what I do?

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-12.md. Yes posts: 88. Near posts: 102.

## Pattern 5, share 12: yes posts

### The parent worries that chores, work or personal time leave the child without enough attention.

10 of 88 posts.

These posts describe the pull of housework, moving home, paid work, study, gaming or a phone against time spent engaging with the child. Children range from 2 months to 1 year, with another post including a 4-year-old sibling; some parents describe attentive care and happy babies while still feeling inadequate. One parent questions a partner's daily gaming and lack of response to the baby's bids for attention.

- "I talk to her and make eye contact and i try my best to avoid container time but i really feel like im negatively impacting her development and attachment by not giving her my undivided attention as much as i feel like i should." [sr093-0058]
- "I have a day off once a week and I feel so guilty if her and I have to do something in town like errands because I feel like I’m wasting time with her. I feel like I’m failing and I don’t even know why. She is my whole entire world and I just feel like I’m not doing enough." [sr010-0161]
- "I find that I have a little amount of time with my daughter, and I feel incredibly guilty because I am struggling to be present with her during the day. I feel like she has a lot of free playtime. Between homework and chores and taking care of her, it’s hard to have lots of time to sit and really focus on her." [sr095-0096]
- "Sometimes he’ll just prop the phone on top of our son’s head so he can still stare at the phone. Mostly he’s just sitting/laying while scrolling his phone or gaming. Sometimes he’ll just lay on the couch and just shout “I’m here” while still gaming when our baby has been fussing for a while. Sometimes he’ll just be ignoring him babbling while he just stares at his phone and our son is just staring at him saying da da da." [sr042-0082]
- "I’m still on leave & physically present with my 3-month old all day, but since my mind is racing, I can feel that I’m not being as present with my baby as I was pre-move & it’s making me so sad & feel like a bad mom." [sr007-0023]
- "Out of interest what’s your daily screen time and how old is your baby? I have a 1 year old and try to be present but am paranoid I’m on my phone too much!" [sr080-0102]

**But:** Several parents describe care and development that do not match their fear of failure. "She is very close to crawling (like literally can basically do it but gives up after like 2 seconds of going), laughs and smiles constantly, loves playing with toys that light up and make noise, so so curious of everything, truly is happy 90% of the day, and sits up fantastic on her own." [sr010-0158]

Posts: 1sge6ll 1shlyun 1sksi2w 1tuxn6c 1u8nhuo 1v83x7g 1vr3k4m 1vut2ci 1wctckj 1x53bbr

### The parent feels guilty when the child plays or waits contentedly without them.

5 of 88 posts.

Parents ask whether quiet waking, independent play or requesting a short break amounts to neglect or rejection. The children range from 5 months to 21 months, and one parent describes similar unattended waking at earlier ages. Some children remain content for half an hour or longer; the oldest actively demands a playmate.

- "what should I do?? am I causing him trauma or brain damage? I read that even being ignored for 10 minutes while in distress can cause babies to have irreparable brain damage that can lead to chronic issues in their adult life." [sr008-0096]
- "My husband thinks that it's fine. I'm worried that we have made her this way by inadvertently neglecting her in some way. We are both big phone users (working on it) but literally as soon as she makes a sound we are interacting with her trying to find out what's wrong. I'm not sure what else I can do when I'm sat next to her and she is playing by herself. I do narrate what she is doing and talk to her or sometimes we listen to music." [sr068-0065]
- "If they wake up from a nap and aren't crying or fussing, do you let them kinda just hang out for a bit by themselves? Do you wait until they start fussing? My LO is 5 months old and will take short naps occasionally but he wakes up usually content to hang for a bit but I feel bad. The second he cries I go to him." [sr049-0051]
- "Buttttt I have a baby cam on, and I just watched her look for me through the mesh wall and I feel so guilty now. She’s safe and she has toys, she’s fed. But this mom guilt is so crazy" [sr049-0169]
- "And just to clarify, I have no problem saying no or setting boundaries as a parent. My concern is whether he’s developmentally ready for that particular boundary. I don’t want to insist that he entertain himself if he’s still at an age where he genuinely needs that constant interaction and reassurance. I guess I’m trying to understand the difference between a toddler being understandably disappointed and a toddler feeling genuinely rejected or disconnected." [sr126-0011]

**But:** A parent explicitly recognises independent play as a welcome freedom. "To be clear, I'm not complaining. I can sit and have a coffee in peace etc.... which I know is a luxury." [sr068-0064]

Posts: 1sjqxn2 1u1ldah 1u539xj 1uvim2b 1x2uvst

### The parent questions whether their balance of stimulation and routine is right for the child.

4 of 88 posts.

The parents question the right amount of activity and structure, from almost 8 weeks through 23 months. They ask what to do in wake windows, whether flexible bedtimes harm the brain, and whether outdoor child-led days compare badly with other families' routines. The request ranges from practical activity ideas to reassurance that their usual way of living is enough.

