- "I let my baby cry for what felt like an eternity (it was no more than 10 minutes). I felt like I was going to throw up. He would cry, then calm himself, then start again. Finally my husband came in and was super upset, and said, “You’re his mom. Why are you letting him cry?” He took over and fed the baby back to sleep while I cried in the hall. I felt so small, and like the time I spent sitting trying to let baby calm himself was for absolutely nothing. When my husband got him back to sleep, I went back in and sat up awake for most of the night." [sr053-0146]
- "I don’t know if you actually read my post, but this is not coming from someone who is an enthusiast about sleep training. This is coming from a severely sleep deprived, depressed, sometimes suicidal mom  who has no support network asides from her husband because our entire family lives in the other side of the planet. I am feeling VERY VERY guilty about needing to do the one thing I promised myself I wouldn’t, something that for me feels very wrong, but I think it might be better for my baby to be sleep trained than to have a mother who’s in complete shambles (parents, actually, my husband is also not doing great)." [sr087-0021]
- "I added an edit to my original post, but I want to make sure to reply to this! I want to clarify what i mean by skill building above, specifically in support of sleep training. Yes, building up the skill of independently falling asleep, but more importantly - recognizing that sleep is critical to healthy development. Sleep deprivation, in whatever form, can harm holistic health. While we're definitely wrestling with the merits of sleep training, that's the piece that is most of merit to me; not that us parents may get more sleep or that routines improve (though those could be nice too), but specifically that I do all I can to provide my kiddo will all the tools he needs to be his healthiest self." [sr017-0053]
- "The first camp is telling me if I don’t do these things my baby will never learn to sleep independently, and I do want that eventually. But the latter camp is telling me that my baby being close to me is necessary for their nervous system to develop and for them to form healthy attachments. And I really want that for him." [sr134-0057]
- "Is there a big difference between him screaming for half an hour in our arms to him screaming in the cot for half an hour but with us being there and still touching him?" [sr003-0008]

**But:** One parent explicitly rejects the claim that a brief bedtime cry means the child has learned not to call for help. "I don’t believe that 15 mins of crying will teach her to stop calling for me when all 23hrs and 45mins of the rest of the day I respond at the drop of a hat. I may be coming across as a bit defensive, because honestly I am. I am doing my best. And hearing that I am teaching my child to not call to me and that I am causing long term damage is a sentiment that is not new in the baby sleep world, it is not true (those findings were based on cases of extreme neglect) and it is hurtful to hear." [sr017-0104]

